- [Retro Announcer 1] Ice cold Coca-Cola.

00:00:35.787 --> 00:00:37.150
- [Retro Announcer 2] A quality product

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:39.133
made with the best of materials.

00:00:41.814 --> 00:00:45.314
♪ Have a Coke and a smile ♪

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:50.820
- So we're here in our little cabin,

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:54.890
which is actually an 1800s church,

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:57.680
see the picture of Jesus right there,

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.050
that we've turned into an
Airbnb that you can rent out

00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:00.883
if you want.

00:01:00.883 --> 00:01:03.313
But I'm here to talk about Coke.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.160
So let me sit here on this
old church Pew and tell you

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.380
what I've learned about this Mexican Coke

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:15.863
over the past few days of
sitting here in isolation.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:19.200
Is this really Mexican Coke?

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:22.280
I mean, it says hecho en Mexico on it,

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.455
so it's gotta be Mexican Coke, right?

00:01:24.455 --> 00:01:25.288
- [Retro Announcer 1] Nothing else

00:01:25.288 --> 00:01:26.410
gives you the bracing sparkle

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:27.650
and the bright little lip

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:31.380
that's so delightfully yours
than ice cold Coca-Cola.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:34.470
- First off, let's talk
about what Coca-Cola even is

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:36.380
and why there's a
difference between US Coke

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:37.370
and Mexican Coke.

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:40.680
I mean, Coca-Cola is like this
giant international company

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.437
that is known for having a secret recipe

00:01:43.437 --> 00:01:46.160
that they export to every
corner of the globe.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:48.965
So why is there a difference
between Coca-Colas?

00:01:48.965 --> 00:01:52.495
♪ I'd like to buy the world a Coke ♪

00:01:52.495 --> 00:01:55.023
♪ And keep it company ♪

00:01:55.023 --> 00:01:58.090
- I mean, let's go straight to
the source of all knowledge:

00:01:58.090 --> 00:01:59.073
Wikipedia, right?

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:05.290
The drink's name refers to
its original ingredients,

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:08.983
coca leaves and kola nuts,
a source of caffeine.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:11.720
Wait, what?

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:13.100
Coca leaves?

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.470
I know coca leaves,

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:17.370
but only because I rode with
a bunch of antinarcotic police

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:21.430
in a helicopter over
coca fields in Columbia,

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:23.905
busting people for cocaine production

00:02:23.905 --> 00:02:26.030
for an episode of Vox Borders.

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:28.356
These are the people who
are growing coca leaves.

00:02:28.356 --> 00:02:29.610
Yes, indeed.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:33.710
The original Coca-Cola
recipe included coca leaves,

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:36.850
which is a derivative that
is used to make cocaine,

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:38.050
but don't worry.

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:41.080
Your bottle of Coke doesn't
include any coca leaves today,

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:43.023
or maybe it does, or no one really knows

00:02:43.023 --> 00:02:44.980
because no one actually knows the recipe.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:47.710
This is not a conspiracy
video about cocaine

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:48.830
being in your Coke.

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:50.320
I'm interested in another ingredient

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:51.950
that is inside of this bottle.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:53.293
And it's the sweetener.

00:02:53.293 --> 00:02:55.100
(sniffing noise)

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:56.471
- It's made out of sugar.

00:02:56.471 --> 00:02:57.304
- Sugar.

00:02:57.304 --> 00:02:58.137
- Sugar.

00:02:58.137 --> 00:02:58.970
- Sugar cane.

00:02:58.970 --> 00:02:59.803
- Sugar!

00:02:59.803 --> 00:03:00.636
- It's made with better ingredients.

00:03:00.636 --> 00:03:02.820
- Originally, all Coca-Cola was sweetened

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:05.130
with the same thing, straight up sugar.

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:08.023
Or what it's chemically known as, sucrose.

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:12.350
But then, a few things
happened in the United States

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:13.200
that changed that.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.060
First, the US government
got really aggressive

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:20.020
about giving money to US
farmers to keep them profitable.

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:23.310
So they started pouring
a lot of money into corn,

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:25.570
subsidizing corn farmers
in the United States.

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:28.180
You can see this very
clearly in our country.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:30.320
There are entire states that are basically

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:32.233
one giant corn field.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:35.740
So if you're a corn farmer in the 1980s,

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:37.070
you have every incentive in the world

00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:39.260
to just produce loads of corn,

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:42.527
as much as your soil can possibly eke out.

00:03:42.527 --> 00:03:44.620
Unsurprisingly, what this means is that

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:47.890
you have a major surplus of corn.

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:50.420
Loads of corn everywhere,
way too much corn

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:53.110
just spilling out of every
corner of the United States.

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:56.320
At the same time, the US is
putting up trade barriers,

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:59.840
basically tariffs, for sugar
coming in from other countries.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:01.970
So sugar became expensive.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:03.860
Corn became incredibly cheap.

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:06.920
And so businesses like
Coca-Cola did what businesses do

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:08.080
and they rejiggered everything

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:09.850
to find the cheapest possible option.

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:14.150
And this is when they turned
to a newly invented sweetener

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:17.748
that came from corn and
tasted sweet like sugar,

00:04:17.748 --> 00:04:19.760
but was chemically very different.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:21.480
Sugar is sucrose.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:22.840
That's its kind of chemical name.

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:26.920
This new corn sweetener
was glucose and fructose,

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:30.032
but it was still very sweet,
but it came from cheap corn.

00:04:30.032 --> 00:04:34.150
And they started to use that
to sweeten their Coca-Cola

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:35.210
in the United States.

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:37.483
Enter high-fructose corn syrup.

00:04:38.329 --> 00:04:40.030
- [Retro Announcer 3]
Did you know that corn,

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:41.800
one of America's favorite foods,

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:43.930
is used to make America's favorite syrup?

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:46.800
A wholesome kind of sweetness
that comes from corn.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.260
- Look what happened to
high-fructose corn syrup production

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:52.810
in the United States versus
sugar in the 80s and 90s.

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:55.600
High fructose corn syrup
became the obvious choice

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:58.330
because it was way cheaper
and Coca-Cola started using it

00:04:58.330 --> 00:04:59.620
to sweeten their drinks.

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:01.810
So this is how you get
two different Coca-Colas.

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:03.670
The United States producing Coke

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.470
that is made with high-fructose corn syrup

00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:07.891
and the rest of the
world, including Mexico,

00:05:07.891 --> 00:05:11.287
without any of these trade
restrictions and corn subsidies,

00:05:11.287 --> 00:05:15.250
continuing to use cane sugar
to sweeten their Coca-Cola.

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:18.114
And then the taste wars begin.

00:05:18.114 --> 00:05:21.197
(funky upbeat music)

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:24.463
- Ooh.

00:05:24.463 --> 00:05:26.090
- It's actually a lot better.

00:05:26.090 --> 00:05:27.150
- [Man Offscreen] It's the sugar.

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:28.328
Got a natural.

00:05:28.328 --> 00:05:29.820
- Not all syrup-y.

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:33.619
- Soon, Mexican Coke became a
delicacy in the United States.

00:05:33.619 --> 00:05:37.390
It was only sent to places that
had big Mexican populations

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:38.560
or other immigrant populations

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:41.350
that were used to this cane sugar Coke.

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:45.790
And so it became rare and
hard to find and obscure.

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:49.960
Not surprisingly, hipsters
like me got really into it.

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:52.890
It was authentic, and it
was the original recipe,

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:54.200
and it was hard to find,

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:56.250
and it was in this retro glass bottle.

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:59.100
It felt like some
commodity was being like,

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:00.760
sent from the other side of the border

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:02.820
in this kind of renegade way

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.090
and we were getting in on it.

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:06.630
It tastes so much better
with that cane sugar.

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:08.169
- [Male Voice] Man, that stuff is so good.

00:06:08.169 --> 00:06:10.913
- It won't feel like corn
syrup all over in my guts.

00:06:10.913 --> 00:06:13.440
- Just have a much smoother,
more delicious taste.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:14.780
- So much better.

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:16.873
So much better.

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:21.420
But wait, this is where it
starts to get really weird.

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:25.550
In 2010, a group of legit
scientists get together

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:27.510
to do a legitimate experiment

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:29.071
for a legitimate science journal.

00:06:29.071 --> 00:06:32.890
And they want to test soft
drinks, including Coca-Cola.

00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:35.060
They want to know what sorts of sweetener

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:37.720
are being used for each and
how much of that sweetener

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:38.553
is in each of them.

00:06:38.553 --> 00:06:39.920
So they gather a bunch of soft drinks,

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:43.700
including a Mexican Coca-Cola
and an American Coca-Cola.

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:45.600
And they do all of their testing.

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:48.945
And what they find is
that the Mexican Coca-Cola

00:06:48.945 --> 00:06:53.945
actually has no sucrose
in it, meaning sugar,

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:58.520
all they find in this
is glucose and fructose.

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:00.606
The elements that are used

00:07:00.606 --> 00:07:03.760
to build high fructose corn syrup.

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:04.593
What?

00:07:04.593 --> 00:07:07.530
In the Mexican Coke that
came from a Latino market

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:11.237
in east LA, they found no sugar.

00:07:11.237 --> 00:07:15.337
They only found the elements
for high fructose corn syrup.

00:07:15.337 --> 00:07:17.590
What is happening?

00:07:17.590 --> 00:07:19.040
This is supposed to be sugar!

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:21.310
It says on the ingredients it's sugar,

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:23.260
but it tastes so much better.

00:07:23.260 --> 00:07:24.220
It's different.

00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:27.907
It tastes so much better, right?

00:07:29.174 --> 00:07:32.174
(suspenseful music)

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:34.457
Better, right?

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:37.240
(sighs) It gets weirder.

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:40.435
In 2013, Mexican lawmakers
became very concerned

00:07:40.435 --> 00:07:43.080
with the obesity rate in Mexico,

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:44.930
which is considered by
public health officials

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:47.740
as quote, "the fattest
country in the world."

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:49.231
Not my words, theirs.

00:07:49.231 --> 00:07:51.900
(dramatic music)

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:53.593
Mexico has a big obesity problem.

00:07:53.593 --> 00:07:56.120
And one of the links to that

00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:59.240
is the massive amount of
sugar that is consumed.

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:02.840
Mexican legislators
slapped on a tax for soda.

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:04.780
All soda was going to be subject to a tax,

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:06.370
which made it more expensive to produce,

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:09.610
which sent Coca-Cola
looking for cheaper options

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:11.894
to bottle their Coke and cheaper options

00:08:11.894 --> 00:08:14.420
equal high fructose corn syrup.

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:16.160
As of 2013.

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:19.150
Okay, we're talking like seven years ago,

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:22.658
more and more bottlers
of Coca Cola in Mexico,

00:08:22.658 --> 00:08:27.650
serving Mexican markets, are
using high fructose corn syrup

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:30.970
to sweeten their Coca-Cola
and selling it in Mexico.

00:08:30.970 --> 00:08:33.500
So now even legitimate Mexican Coke

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:35.760
is not even Mexican Coke anymore.

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:38.810
It's identical to the high
fructose corn syrup stuff

00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:40.003
you buy here.

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:46.493
One of the main bottlers came
out and said, don't worry.

00:08:46.493 --> 00:08:49.772
We're going to continue
to send cane sugar Coke

00:08:49.772 --> 00:08:52.220
to the United States because we know

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:54.570
that people really like
it in the United States

00:08:54.570 --> 00:08:57.610
and we'll to send it because
it's good business for us

00:08:57.610 --> 00:08:59.040
to keep making it with cane sugar.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:01.670
But I don't even believe that
guy because of that study.

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:03.800
Remember the study that said no sucrose,

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:05.533
only glucose and fructose.

00:09:06.867 --> 00:09:08.810
Ie. high fructose corn syrup.

00:09:08.810 --> 00:09:10.520
I don't believe you, Mexican bottler.

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:11.740
I don't believe that you're sending us

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:13.080
cane sugar Coca-Cola anymore.

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:15.880
Not surprisingly, Coca-Cola
has caught onto this idea

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:18.104
that people like me love Mexican Coke

00:09:18.104 --> 00:09:20.150
and they've totally leaned into it.

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:22.640
They've created this gimmicky branding

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:24.090
that's like, hecho en Mexico.

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:26.310
Here it is.

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:28.730
Here's the offending product.

00:09:28.730 --> 00:09:30.680
Bought it at target.

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:34.900
It's just sitting here,
being so tokenizing

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:37.220
of Mexican aesthetic and culture.

00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:38.536
Okay, back to it.

00:09:38.536 --> 00:09:41.370
Authentic original flavors.

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:42.650
And they've really leaned into it

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:44.410
because they know that
there's a cult following

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:46.218
of people like me, who
will pay more money.

00:09:46.218 --> 00:09:48.530
Okay, so I've been social
distancing out here

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:50.640
in this cabin in the
woods for like two weeks.

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:52.930
I have been reading way too much

00:09:52.930 --> 00:09:56.500
about the Coca-Cola bottling industry.

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:59.580
And it has completely
shattered my identity

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:01.314
as a Mexican Coke drinker.

00:10:01.314 --> 00:10:04.360
One because there's probably
not even sugar in this,

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:05.930
and two because Mexicans

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:08.440
don't even drink Mexican Coke anymore.

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:10.010
They drink high fructose corn syrup.

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:10.843
Cool.

00:10:10.843 --> 00:10:12.463
I guess there is still one reason

00:10:12.463 --> 00:10:14.942
why I would drink Mexican Coke,

00:10:14.942 --> 00:10:19.942
Because it still comes in
this cool retro glass bottle

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:24.050
that makes me feel
really cool about myself.

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:25.630
And that is why I will
continue to drink it.

